1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,769
This is surgecast: a handheld guide that
finds the source of a smell, the way a moth

2
00:00:05,769 --> 00:00:06,089
does.

3
00:00:07,189 --> 00:00:11,113
A Raspberry Pi reads three sensors on a
single I²C bus.

4
00:00:11,463 --> 00:00:15,190
A gas sensor sits on a forward boom, upwind
of the Pi's heat.

5
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:20,263
A wind vane on a magnetic angle sensor reads
which way the air is moving, and an IMU

6
00:00:20,263 --> 00:00:22,007
tracks which way you're facing.

7
00:00:22,357 --> 00:00:26,350
A motor in the handle buzzes each cue, so
you feel it instead of reading it.

8
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:30,190
When odor arrives, it tells you to walk into
the wind.

9
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:35,103
When the odor is lost, it has you cast
across the wind until you find it again.

10
00:00:36,203 --> 00:00:40,949
Here's a simulated walk through a box-fan
plume, with a slow gas sensor and a person

11
00:00:40,949 --> 00:00:42,136
reacting to each cue.

12
00:00:42,486 --> 00:00:46,816
Red stretches are surges upwind. Blue
zigzags are casts across the wind.

13
00:00:47,166 --> 00:00:51,993
The slow sensor's level barely changes, so
the guide fires on odor onsets, the

14
00:00:51,993 --> 00:00:53,912
shaded marks, not on the level.

15
00:00:54,262 --> 00:00:57,930
In this run, the walker reaches the source
in about 11 seconds.

16
00:00:59,030 --> 00:01:03,396
The design and the simulation are open
source. The sensors haven't been built yet.
